# Minimal image for the Railway *cron-only* drift service (not the web app).
# Cloned from Dockerfile.devbugs-cron: a curl one-shot that pokes the Flask
# webhook and exits. Fires POST /v2/internal/drift/run weekly; the backend
# computes PSI + the p-chart and returns the PM-3 2x2 (Appendix G.5/G.7).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, given bin/railway-drift-cron.sh also runs fine
# as a plain Start Command on a Railpack build: Railway's builder detection
# sees this repo's Dockerfile.* siblings and auto-selects the Dockerfile
# builder. An EMPTY Dockerfile Path then resolves to ./Dockerfile, which this
# repo does not have (only the suffixed per-cron ones), and the build dies
# with "couldn't locate a dockerfile at path Dockerfile in code archive".
# Pinning an explicit path is deterministic; relying on auto-detection is not.
#
# Railway: New service → connect repo → Settings:
#   Builder:          Dockerfile
#   Dockerfile path:  Dockerfile.drift-cron
#   Custom Start Command:  *** LEAVE EMPTY ***  (see the trap below)
#   Cron Schedule:    0 6 * * 1     (06:00 UTC Mondays)
#
# ⚠️ THE TRAP, and it is the one that actually bit. Do NOT set a Dockerfile
# path AND a Custom Start Command together. The image below contains exactly
# one file — /app/run.sh — and no bin/ directory, so a start command reading
# `sh bin/railway-drift-cron.sh` fails with "sh: can't open ...". Pick ONE:
#   A) Builder Railpack + Start Command `sh bin/railway-drift-cron.sh`, or
#   B) this Dockerfile with NO start command (the ENTRYPOINT already runs it).
# Worse still is pointing a drift service at Dockerfile.life-reminders-cron:
# with the start command then cleared, its ENTRYPOINT would quietly send LIFE
# REMINDERS to real users on the drift schedule.
#
# Variables for this service:
#   DRIFT_BACKEND_URL     https://flask-backend-production-ab37.up.railway.app
#                         (or https://dev.willpowerlab.com — same service, two
#                          custom domains; the FE uses the railway.app one)
#   DRIFT_MONITOR_SECRET  same value as on the Flask WEB service
# Optional:
#   DRIFT_WEEKS           lookback window, default 4 (the appendix's figure)
#
# The route is DEAD BY DEFAULT: without DRIFT_MONITOR_SECRET on the WEB
# service it returns 503, so this cron fails loudly rather than looking
# healthy while measuring nothing.
#
# AC-9: the response carries PSI values, fire rates and control limits. This
# is an internal audit surface; none of it may reach a user-facing surface.

FROM curlimages/curl:8.20.0
WORKDIR /app
COPY bin/railway-drift-cron.sh /app/run.sh
ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/sh", "/app/run.sh"]
